# Microservices patterns added: Fan-out + Idempotency + DLQ

## IMPORTANT first step
DELETE the old `OrderConsumer.java` — it's replaced by `InventoryConsumer.java`.
(Keeping both would double-register the inventory-service group.)

## Files now in the project
```
src/main/java/com/example/demo/
├── OrderController.java       producer (now accepts optional orderId + item=poison)
├── InventoryConsumer.java     consumer #1 — idempotent, can fail -> DLQ
├── NotificationConsumer.java  consumer #2 — fan-out (own group)
├── DeliveryConsumer.java      consumer #3 — fan-out (own group)
├── DeadLetterConsumer.java    watches order-confirmed.DLT
├── KafkaErrorConfig.java      retry (2x) then publish to DLQ
├── KafkaTopicConfig.java      creates order-confirmed topic
├── DemoApplication.java       (unchanged)
└── HelloController.java       (unchanged)
```
No new dependency needed — spring-kafka already includes retry + DLQ support.

## Run
```bash
docker compose up -d        # Kafka broker
./gradlew bootRun           # the app
```

## Demo 1 — FAN-OUT (one event, three services)
```bash
curl -X POST "http://localhost:8080/orders?item=milk"
```
App console shows ALL THREE independent consumers react to the one event:
```
[inventory] decremented stock  orderId=ab12cd34  offset=0
[notification] sent push/SMS  orderId=ab12cd34
[delivery] assigning rider  orderId=ab12cd34
```
Why: each consumer has a different groupId, so each group gets its own copy of every
message. That is publish-once, consume-many — the post-order microservices pattern.

## Demo 2 — IDEMPOTENCY (duplicate handling)
Send the SAME orderId twice:
```bash
curl -X POST "http://localhost:8080/orders?item=milk&orderId=DUP-1"
curl -X POST "http://localhost:8080/orders?item=milk&orderId=DUP-1"
```
Inventory processes it once, then skips the repeat:
```
[inventory] decremented stock  orderId=DUP-1  offset=...
[inventory] DUPLICATE skipped  orderId=DUP-1
```
Why it matters: Kafka is at-least-once by default, so a consumer CAN see a message
twice. Idempotent consumers make re-delivery safe (no double stock decrement).

## Demo 3 — DLQ (poison message + resilience)
```bash
curl -X POST "http://localhost:8080/orders?item=poison"
```
Inventory throws, the error handler retries twice, then parks it in the dead-letter topic:
```
[notification] sent push/SMS  orderId=...       <- other services still succeed
[delivery] assigning rider  orderId=...
... (inventory retries and fails) ...
[DLQ] poison parked  orderId=...  value={"orderId":"...","item":"poison"}
```
Why it matters: a poison message would otherwise block the partition forever
(head-of-line blocking). Retry + DLQ keeps the pipeline moving and parks the bad
record for investigation. Note other services are unaffected — failure is isolated.

## Interview gold (say all three)
- "Fan-out: the order service publishes once; inventory, notification, and delivery
   are separate consumer groups that each get the event and scale independently."
- "Idempotency: at-least-once delivery means duplicates happen, so consumers dedupe
   on a correlation id — re-processing must not double-apply."
- "DLQ: I retry with backoff, then route poison messages to a dead-letter topic so one
   bad record can't block the partition, and as an SDET I explicitly test that DLQ path."